From aeafa60e58122ea7033741398078d7ad1f301665 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Majid Arasskhani <M.Arasskhani@gosilkroad.net>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 21:38:16 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 02/12] feat(budget): money columns become NUMERIC;
 reserved-counter drift is measured
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Balances, holds, credit limits and per-request costs on users,
alpha_router_api_keys, budget_plans, budget_reservations and request_logs
were `double precision`; catalog prices on ai_models too. Every SQL-side
increment accumulated binary rounding error, which is the reason the
reserved-counter repair job exists.

- `app.database.MoneyUSD = Numeric(20, 12, asdecimal=False)` and
  `UnitPriceUSD = Numeric(24, 14, asdecimal=False)`; fifteen columns switch
  to them. `asdecimal=False` keeps every caller on float — no code outside
  the models changes; the ledger tables (already Numeric/Decimal) stay the
  source of truth.
- Revision 5c1d2e3f4a5b: `ALTER COLUMN ... TYPE NUMERIC USING ROUND(col::numeric,
  scale)` on PostgreSQL (SQLite is untyped and skipped). Idempotent by nature.
- `reconcile_drifted_reserved_counters` now reports what it found: gauge
  `alpharouter_budget_reserved_drift_usd` (0 when counters were exact) and
  event `budget_reserved_drift_repaired` per subject. Plan 4.2 says the
  repair job can be retired after a month at zero — this is the instrument.
- tests/test_money_numeric_postgres.py (canary): 1000 × 0.001 increments
  land on exactly "1.000000000000", the ORM reads 1.0 as float, and the
  reconciliation finds no drift.

Verified: existing database upgraded in place, drift gate 0; full suite
1371 passed; canary 13 passed.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UTnpHajSmkk2JMLfuQoV7o
